ADDART({id:"ganymede",t:"Ganymede",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌑",w:"Ganymede (moon)",r:["jupiter","io","europa","callisto","junoprobe"],
p:[["The biggest moon","Ganymede is the largest moon in the solar system."],["Bigger than Mercury","It is wider than the planet Mercury."],["A magnetic moon","It is the only moon known to have its own magnetic field."]],
l:"Ganymede is the largest of Jupiter’s moons and the largest moon in the solar system, about 5,270 kilometers across, wider than the planet Mercury though less massive. Discovered by Galileo Galilei in January 1610, it is one of the four Galilean moons. Ganymede is made of roughly equal amounts of rock and ice, with an iron-rich core. It is the only moon known to generate its own magnetic field, which creates auroras around its poles. Scientists believe a deep ocean of salty water lies hidden between layers of ice beneath its surface, possibly holding more water than all of Earth’s oceans. Ganymede has been studied by Voyager, Galileo and Juno, and the European Space Agency’s JUICE mission is on its way to orbit it in the 2030s.",
s:[["Surface","Ganymede’s surface has two main types of terrain: dark, ancient regions covered with craters, and lighter regions crossed by long grooves and ridges. The grooved terrain likely formed as the icy crust stretched and cracked long ago. Bright craters show where impacts exposed fresh ice."],["Magnetic field","In 1996 the Galileo spacecraft discovered that Ganymede has its own magnetic field, probably produced by movements in its molten iron core. This field sits inside Jupiter’s much larger magnetic field and produces glowing auroras around Ganymede’s poles."],["A hidden ocean","In 2015 astronomers used the Hubble Space Telescope to watch Ganymede’s auroras shift back and forth as Jupiter’s magnetic field changed. The amount of rocking suggested that a large, salty ocean beneath the surface was dampening the motion. The ocean may lie about 150 kilometers below the icy crust."],["Size and structure","Ganymede is slightly larger than Saturn’s moon Titan and about 8 percent wider than Mercury, though only about half as massive because it contains so much ice. If it orbited the Sun instead of Jupiter, it might be considered a planet."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Diameter","About 5,270 km"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo"],["Special feature","Its own magnetic field"],["Mission","ESA’s JUICE"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo discovers Ganymede"],["1979","Voyager 1 and 2 photograph Ganymede"],["1996","Galileo discovers Ganymede’s magnetic field"],["2015","Hubble finds evidence of an underground ocean"],["2021","Juno flies close past Ganymede"],["2023","ESA launches the JUICE mission to Jupiter’s icy moons"]],
s2:[["JUICE","The European Space Agency’s Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer, called JUICE, launched in April 2023. After flybys of Earth, Venus and the Moon to gain speed, it is due to reach Jupiter in 2031, study Europa, Callisto and Ganymede, and then go into orbit around Ganymede in 2034, becoming the first spacecraft to orbit a moon other than our own."],["Juno’s visit","On June 7, 2021, NASA’s Juno spacecraft flew about 1,000 kilometers above Ganymede, the closest any spacecraft had come in over two decades. It took detailed images of the surface and measured its ice shell and magnetic field."],["Naming","Ganymede is named after a figure in Greek mythology, a beautiful youth carried to Mount Olympus by Zeus, who in Roman mythology is Jupiter. The names of Jupiter’s large moons were proposed by the German astronomer Simon Marius, who claimed to have discovered them around the same time as Galileo."],["Could it host life?","Because Ganymede probably has a large saltwater ocean, scientists consider it a possible place for life. However, its ocean may be sandwiched between layers of ice, rather than touching a rocky seafloor, which could limit the chemical energy available. JUICE will help answer how habitable its ocean might be."],["Seen without a telescope?","Some historians suggest that the Chinese astronomer Gan De may have seen a faint star next to Jupiter around 365 BC, possibly Ganymede, nearly 2,000 years before Galileo. Under very dark skies, Ganymede is bright enough in theory to be seen without a telescope if Jupiter’s glare is blocked."],["Seeing it from the Philippines","With binoculars or a small telescope, Jupiter’s four Galilean moons, including Ganymede, can be seen from the Philippines as tiny points of light lined up beside the planet. Their positions change from night to night as they orbit Jupiter, just as Galileo saw in 1610."]],
sp:["Galileo’s wandering stars","In January 1610 Galileo Galilei pointed his homemade telescope at Jupiter and noticed three small stars lined up near the planet. The next night, they had moved. Over the following nights, he saw a fourth, and realized that these were not stars at all but moons orbiting Jupiter. This showed that not everything in the heavens revolved around Earth, supporting the Sun-centered system of Copernicus. Galileo named them the Medicean Stars in honor of the powerful Medici family. Today they are called the Galilean moons, and Ganymede, the largest, is now known to be bigger than the planet Mercury."],
g:[["Galilean moons","Jupiter’s four largest moons"],["Magnetic field","A region of magnetic force around an object"],["Aurora","Glowing light caused by charged particles"],["JUICE","A European mission to Jupiter’s icy moons"]],
f:["Ganymede is the largest moon in the solar system.","It is bigger than Mercury.","It has its own magnetic field."],
fun:"Ganymede may hold more water in its hidden ocean than all of Earth’s oceans combined."});

ADDART({id:"callisto",t:"Callisto",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌑",w:"Callisto (moon)",r:["jupiter","ganymede","europa","io","moons"],
p:[["Battered moon","Callisto has one of the most heavily cratered surfaces in the solar system."],["Ancient surface","Its surface has changed little for billions of years."],["Future base?","Scientists have imagined a human base on Callisto."]],
l:"Callisto is the second-largest moon of Jupiter and the third-largest moon in the solar system, about 4,820 kilometers across, nearly the size of Mercury. Discovered by Galileo Galilei in 1610, it is the outermost of Jupiter’s four large Galilean moons. Callisto’s dark, icy surface is one of the oldest and most heavily cratered in the solar system, showing that it has had little geological activity for billions of years. Unlike Io, Europa and Ganymede, Callisto is not locked in an orbital rhythm with the other moons and experiences little tidal heating. Even so, measurements by the Galileo spacecraft suggested that it may have a salty ocean deep beneath its crust. Because it lies outside Jupiter’s strongest radiation, it has been proposed as a site for a future human base.",
s:[["Craters everywhere","Callisto’s surface is covered in craters of every size, packed so tightly that new impacts often overlap old ones. Its largest feature, Valhalla, is a huge impact basin surrounded by rings of ridges extending about 1,800 kilometers from its center, like ripples frozen in ice."],["Dark and bright","Callisto’s surface is a mix of dark material and ice. Over time, ice has evaporated from some areas, leaving dark dusty deposits, while bright ice remains on crater rims and slopes. This process gives it a mottled look."],["A quiet interior","Callisto appears to be only partly separated into layers, with rock and ice mixed through much of its interior. This suggests it never became hot enough to fully melt, unlike Ganymede. Its lack of tidal heating is one reason it is so geologically quiet."],["A possible ocean","When the Galileo spacecraft flew past Callisto, it detected disturbances in Jupiter’s magnetic field that could be explained by a layer of salty, electrically conducting water beneath the surface. Scientists are still debating whether this ocean exists and how deep it might be."]],
k:[["Planet","Jupiter"],["Diameter","About 4,820 km"],["Discovered","1610, by Galileo"],["Largest feature","Valhalla basin"],["Notable","Among the most cratered surfaces known"]],
d:[["1610","Galileo discovers Callisto"],["1979","Voyager spacecraft photograph Callisto"],["1996","Galileo begins close flybys of Callisto"],["1998","Galileo data suggest a possible ocean"],["2003","A NASA study considers a human base on Callisto"],["2023","JUICE launches with plans to fly past Callisto many times"]],
s2:[["A base on Callisto","In 2003 a NASA study explored the idea of sending astronauts to Callisto sometime in the future. Because Callisto orbits outside Jupiter’s intense radiation belts, people there would receive much less harmful radiation than on Io or Europa. Its ice could provide water and fuel. Such a mission remains a distant possibility."],["Named for a myth","In Greek mythology, Callisto was a nymph loved by Zeus, who was turned into a bear and placed in the sky as the constellation Ursa Major, the Great Bear. Many of Callisto’s craters and features are named after figures from Norse and other northern myths, such as Valhalla and Asgard."],["Studying ancient history","Because Callisto’s surface has hardly changed, its craters record the history of impacts in the outer solar system over billions of years. Scientists compare its crater counts with those on other moons to estimate the ages of their surfaces."],["JUICE flybys","The European Space Agency’s JUICE mission plans to make many close flybys of Callisto in the early 2030s before orbiting Ganymede. It will map Callisto’s surface, search for evidence of its ocean and study its thin atmosphere of carbon dioxide."],["The odd one out","Io, Europa and Ganymede are linked in a regular pattern of orbits that causes tidal heating, but Callisto orbits farther out and is not part of this rhythm. This helps explain why Io is volcanic, Europa and Ganymede are geologically active, and Callisto is quiet."],["A thin atmosphere","Callisto has an extremely thin atmosphere of carbon dioxide and possibly oxygen, detected by the Galileo spacecraft and the Hubble Space Telescope. It is far too thin to breathe, but it shows that even small, quiet moons can have surprising features."]],
sp:["A moon for astronauts","In 2003 a team of NASA scientists and engineers studied how humans might one day explore the outer solar system. They considered Jupiter’s moons and concluded that Callisto would be the best place for a base. Its distance from Jupiter keeps radiation levels relatively low, its surface is stable, and its ice could be melted for water and split into oxygen and hydrogen. From a base on Callisto, robots could be controlled in real time on Europa, searching its ocean for life. The study imagined a mission perhaps decades in the future, using advanced nuclear-powered spacecraft. For now, Callisto remains a quiet, ancient world waiting to be explored."],
g:[["Crater","A bowl-shaped hole made by an impact"],["Impact basin","A very large crater"],["Tidal heating","Heat produced by gravitational squeezing"],["Galilean moon","One of Jupiter’s four largest moons"]],
f:["Callisto is heavily cratered.","It is the third-largest moon in the solar system.","It may have a hidden ocean."],
fun:"The rings around Callisto’s Valhalla basin reach about 1,800 kilometers from its center."});

ADDART({id:"triton",t:"Triton",c:"Astronomy",i:"❄️",w:"Triton (moon)",r:["uranep","pluto","kuiper","voyager","moons"],
p:[["Neptune’s giant moon","Triton is Neptune’s largest moon."],["Going backward","It orbits in the opposite direction to Neptune’s spin."],["Icy geysers","Voyager 2 saw dark plumes erupting from its surface."]],
l:"Triton is the largest moon of Neptune, about 2,700 kilometers across, slightly smaller than Earth’s Moon. Discovered in 1846 by the British astronomer William Lassell just 17 days after Neptune itself was found, it is unusual because it orbits Neptune backward, in the opposite direction to the planet’s rotation. This suggests that Triton did not form around Neptune but was captured from the Kuiper Belt, the region of icy bodies beyond Neptune, making it a cousin of Pluto. Triton’s surface is covered with frozen nitrogen and is one of the coldest places measured in the solar system, around minus 235 degrees Celsius. When Voyager 2 flew past in 1989, it discovered geyser-like plumes rising kilometers high, showing that this frozen world is still active.",
s:[["A captured moon","Most large moons orbit in the same direction their planets spin, as they formed from the same disk of material. Triton’s backward, or retrograde, orbit indicates it was probably once a dwarf planet in the Kuiper Belt that came too close to Neptune and was captured. The capture would have disrupted Neptune’s original moons."],["Cold surface","Triton’s surface is made mostly of frozen nitrogen, with water ice, methane and carbon dioxide. Its high reflectivity sends much of the faint sunlight back into space, keeping it extremely cold. Parts of its surface have a wrinkled pattern nicknamed cantaloupe terrain, after the melon’s rough skin."],["Geysers","Voyager 2 photographed dark streaks and columns of material rising about 8 kilometers above Triton’s surface. Scientists think sunlight warms nitrogen ice beneath a transparent layer, building pressure until gas bursts out, carrying dark dust. Others suggest heat from within may be involved."],["A doomed orbit","Triton is slowly spiraling inward because of tidal interactions with Neptune. In perhaps a few billion years, it may come so close that Neptune’s gravity tears it apart, possibly forming a spectacular ring system like Saturn’s."]],
k:[["Planet","Neptune"],["Diameter","About 2,700 km"],["Discovered","1846, by William Lassell"],["Orbit","Retrograde"],["Surface temperature","About −235 °C"]],
d:[["1846","Neptune is discovered on September 23"],["1846","William Lassell discovers Triton on October 10"],["1949","Gerard Kuiper discovers Neptune’s moon Nereid"],["1989","Voyager 2 flies past Triton and sees geysers"],["2019","A mission concept called Trident is proposed to visit Triton"],["2020s","Scientists study Triton with powerful telescopes from Earth"]],
s2:[["Voyager 2’s final world","On August 25, 1989, Voyager 2 flew past Neptune and, hours later, Triton, the last world it visited before heading toward interstellar space. It passed about 40,000 kilometers from Triton and took images of about 40 percent of its surface. The images revealed young, smooth plains, few craters and active plumes, surprising scientists who expected a dead, frozen world."],["Lassell’s discovery","William Lassell was a wealthy brewer from Liverpool who built his own powerful telescopes as a hobby. After Neptune was discovered in September 1846, he turned his telescope toward it and, on October 10, found its large moon. The moon was not named Triton until much later, after the son of the sea god Poseidon in Greek mythology."],["A young surface","Triton has very few craters, which means its surface has been renewed relatively recently. Scientists think icy volcanism, or cryovolcanism, in which liquids such as water and ammonia erupt instead of lava, may have reshaped it. Some scientists suggest it could have an ocean beneath its crust."],["Proposed missions","No spacecraft has returned to Triton since 1989. Scientists proposed a low-cost NASA mission called Trident to fly past Triton and study its plumes and possible ocean, but it was not selected in 2021. Other concepts, such as orbiters for Neptune, continue to be discussed."],["Pluto’s cousin","Because Triton likely came from the Kuiper Belt, scientists compare it with Pluto, which has a similar size, density and surface ices. New Horizons’ 2015 images of Pluto revealed many features that helped scientists better understand Triton."],["Seasons on Triton","Neptune takes about 165 years to orbit the Sun, and Triton’s tilted orbit gives it extreme seasons, each lasting decades. As seasons change, nitrogen ice may move between the poles, changing Triton’s thin atmosphere. Observations from Earth have shown that its atmosphere has changed since 1989."]],
sp:["Geysers on the coldest moon","When Voyager 2’s images of Triton arrived on Earth in August 1989, scientists expected to see a frozen, inactive world. Instead, they noticed dozens of dark streaks on the pinkish surface, all pointing in the same direction, like smoke blown by the wind. Looking more closely, they found narrow columns of dark material rising straight up about 8 kilometers before being carried sideways by the thin atmosphere. These were geysers, erupting on a moon colder than almost anywhere else ever measured. The discovery showed that even the most distant and frigid worlds can be surprisingly active, and Triton became one of Voyager’s most memorable surprises."],
g:[["Retrograde orbit","An orbit in the opposite direction to a planet’s spin"],["Kuiper Belt","A region of icy bodies beyond Neptune"],["Cryovolcanism","Eruptions of icy materials"],["Geyser","A jet of gas or liquid bursting from the ground"]],
f:["Triton orbits Neptune backward.","It was probably captured from the Kuiper Belt.","Voyager 2 saw geysers on Triton."],
fun:"Triton’s surface is one of the coldest places ever measured in the solar system."});

ADDART({id:"ceres",t:"Ceres",c:"Astronomy",i:"🪨",w:"Ceres (dwarf planet)",r:["asteroidbelt","dwarfplanets","pluto","comet","solar"],
p:[["Dwarf planet","Ceres is the largest object in the asteroid belt."],["First found","It was the first asteroid ever discovered, in 1801."],["Shining salts","The Dawn spacecraft found mysterious bright spots on its surface."]],
l:"Ceres is the largest object in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, about 940 kilometers across, and the only dwarf planet in the inner solar system. Discovered on January 1, 1801, by the Italian astronomer Giuseppe Piazzi, it was at first called a planet, then classed as an asteroid for about 150 years, and in 2006 reclassified as a dwarf planet, alongside Pluto. Ceres is round, made of rock mixed with ice and salts, and may contain pockets of salty liquid water beneath its surface. NASA’s Dawn spacecraft orbited Ceres from 2015 to 2018, discovering bright deposits of salts in its craters and a lonely ice volcano named Ahuna Mons. Ceres makes up about a quarter of the mass of the entire asteroid belt.",
s:[["Discovery","On the first night of 1801, Giuseppe Piazzi, observing from Palermo in Sicily, spotted a faint object that moved from night to night. He tracked it for weeks before it disappeared in the Sun’s glare. The young German mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss developed a new method to calculate its orbit, allowing astronomers to find it again at the end of 1801."],["Planet, asteroid, dwarf planet","Ceres was first considered a new planet. But as more objects were found in the same region, astronomers began calling them asteroids, meaning star-like. In 2006 the International Astronomical Union created the category of dwarf planet, which includes round bodies that have not cleared their orbits, and Ceres qualified."],["Bright spots","As Dawn approached in 2015, it photographed puzzling bright spots in Occator crater. Closer views showed they were deposits of sodium carbonate and other salts, left behind when salty water rose from below and evaporated. This suggested that a reservoir of brine may exist beneath the crater."],["Ahuna Mons","Dawn also found Ahuna Mons, a lone mountain about 4 kilometers high with steep sides, thought to be a cryovolcano built by eruptions of salty, muddy ice. Its fresh-looking slopes suggest it formed relatively recently in geological terms."]],
k:[["Location","Asteroid belt"],["Diameter","About 940 km"],["Discovered","January 1, 1801"],["Discoverer","Giuseppe Piazzi"],["Spacecraft visit","Dawn, 2015–2018"]],
d:[["1801","Giuseppe Piazzi discovers Ceres"],["1801","Gauss calculates Ceres’s orbit, allowing it to be found again"],["1802","Pallas, a second asteroid, is discovered"],["2006","Ceres is reclassified as a dwarf planet"],["2015","The Dawn spacecraft enters orbit around Ceres"],["2018","Dawn’s mission ends as it runs out of fuel"]],
s2:[["The Dawn mission","NASA’s Dawn spacecraft, launched in 2007, used efficient ion engines to visit two large bodies of the asteroid belt. It orbited the protoplanet Vesta from 2011 to 2012, then traveled to Ceres, arriving in March 2015. It was the first spacecraft to orbit two different bodies beyond Earth. It ran out of fuel in 2018 and remains in a stable orbit around Ceres."],["Water and organics","Dawn found evidence that Ceres contains much water ice and hydrated minerals, as well as organic compounds in some areas. Scientists think Ceres may once have had a global ocean, parts of which may survive as briny pockets deep underground. This makes Ceres interesting in the search for habitable environments."],["Named for a goddess","Piazzi named Ceres after the Roman goddess of agriculture and grain, from whose name the word cereal comes. The element cerium, discovered in 1803, was named after the dwarf planet. Features on Ceres are named after gods and festivals of harvests and agriculture from cultures around the world."],["The search for a missing planet","In the late 1700s, astronomers noticed a pattern in the distances of planets from the Sun, called the Titius–Bode rule, which predicted a planet between Mars and Jupiter. When Ceres was found at about the right distance, many believed the missing planet had been discovered. The rule later turned out to be a coincidence."],["Too small to see","Ceres is too faint to be seen with the naked eye except under extremely dark skies at its brightest, but it can be observed with binoculars or a small telescope when it is well placed. Astronomy enthusiasts in the Philippines sometimes track it using star charts and apps."],["Future exploration","Scientists have proposed missions to return samples from Ceres’s bright salt deposits or to land near Occator crater. Such missions would help determine whether Ceres still has liquid water and what chemistry takes place inside it."]],
sp:["Lost and found","After discovering Ceres on January 1, 1801, Giuseppe Piazzi followed it for six weeks before illness and the object’s approach to the Sun interrupted his observations. Astronomers across Europe wanted to find it again, but with so few observations, no one could predict where it would reappear. Carl Friedrich Gauss, only 24 years old, developed a new mathematical method for calculating orbits from limited data. He predicted where Ceres would be months later. On December 31, 1801, almost exactly a year after its discovery, astronomers found Ceres very close to where Gauss said it would be. His methods became important tools in science and statistics."],
g:[["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Asteroid belt","A region of rocky objects between Mars and Jupiter"],["Cryovolcano","A volcano that erupts icy materials"],["Brine","Very salty water"]],
f:["Ceres was the first asteroid discovered.","It is a dwarf planet.","Dawn found salt deposits on its surface."],
fun:"The word cereal comes from Ceres, the Roman goddess the dwarf planet is named after."});

ADDART({id:"alphacentauri",t:"Alpha Centauri",c:"Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Alpha Centauri",r:["stars","exoplanet","binarystars","distances","astrobio","proximab"],
p:[["Our nearest neighbors","Alpha Centauri is the closest star system to the Sun."],["Three stars","It includes two Sun-like stars and the red dwarf Proxima Centauri."],["A nearby planet","Proxima Centauri has at least one Earth-sized planet."]],
l:"Alpha Centauri is the closest star system to our solar system, about 4.3 light-years away, in the southern constellation of Centaurus. It consists of three stars: Alpha Centauri A and B, two stars similar to the Sun that orbit each other every 80 years, and Proxima Centauri, a small, dim red dwarf that is the nearest single star to the Sun at about 4.24 light-years. To the naked eye, A and B appear as one bright star, the third-brightest in the night sky. In 2016 astronomers discovered an Earth-sized planet, Proxima b, orbiting Proxima Centauri in its habitable zone, where liquid water might exist. Alpha Centauri is a popular target for imagined interstellar voyages and is visible low in the southern sky from the Philippines.",
s:[["Alpha Centauri A and B","Alpha Centauri A is slightly larger and brighter than the Sun, while B is slightly smaller and cooler. They orbit each other in an elongated orbit, coming as close as Saturn is to the Sun and moving apart as far as Pluto is from the Sun. Together they shine brighter than any star except Sirius and Canopus."],["Proxima Centauri","Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf about one-eighth the mass of the Sun and far too faint to see without a telescope. It orbits the pair A and B at a great distance, taking hundreds of thousands of years. It frequently erupts with powerful flares that may strip atmospheres from nearby planets."],["Proxima b","Proxima b, discovered in 2016 by detecting tiny wobbles in Proxima Centauri’s light, has at least about 1.1 times Earth’s mass and orbits its star every 11 days. It lies in the habitable zone, but intense flares and radiation from its star may make it hostile to life. Another small planet, Proxima d, was reported in 2022."],["How far is 4.3 light-years?","Light from Alpha Centauri takes over four years to reach us. The fastest spacecraft would take thousands of years to get there. If the Sun were the size of a grape in Manila, Alpha Centauri would be like another pair of grapes in another country hundreds of kilometers away."]],
k:[["Distance","About 4.3 light-years"],["Stars","Alpha Centauri A, B and Proxima"],["Nearest single star","Proxima Centauri"],["Known planets","Proxima b and others"],["Constellation","Centaurus"]],
d:[["1689","Jean Richaud notices that Alpha Centauri is a double star"],["1839","Thomas Henderson’s measurement of its distance is published"],["1915","Robert Innes discovers Proxima Centauri"],["2016","Astronomers announce the planet Proxima b"],["2016","The Breakthrough Starshot project is announced"],["2022","A smaller planet, Proxima d, is reported"]],
s2:[["Measuring the distance","In 1832 and 1833 the Scottish astronomer Thomas Henderson, working at the Cape of Good Hope in South Africa, measured the tiny shift in Alpha Centauri’s position as Earth orbited the Sun, called parallax. He published his result in 1839, shortly after Friedrich Bessel published a distance for the star 61 Cygni. These were among the first measurements of distances to stars, revealing how vast space is."],["Breakthrough Starshot","In 2016 a group of scientists and investors announced Breakthrough Starshot, a proposal to send tiny, gram-sized spacecraft attached to thin light sails toward Alpha Centauri. Powerful lasers on Earth would accelerate them to a fraction of the speed of light, allowing them to arrive within about 20 years. The idea faces enormous technical challenges and remains a long-term research project."],["Seeing it from the Philippines","Alpha Centauri lies far south in the sky, near the Southern Cross. From the Philippines, it can be seen low above the southern horizon on evenings from about March to July, especially from the southern islands. It and its neighbor Beta Centauri are called the Pointers because they point toward the Southern Cross."],["Searching for more planets","Astronomers continue to search for planets around Alpha Centauri A and B. In 2025 astronomers using the James Webb Space Telescope reported a possible giant planet around Alpha Centauri A, which still needs to be confirmed. Any planet in the habitable zone of these Sun-like stars would be a major target for future study."],["In science fiction","Alpha Centauri is a favorite destination in science fiction, from novels and films to video games. The Chinese science fiction novel The Three-Body Problem by Liu Cixin, later adapted for television, imagines a civilization in a three-star system inspired by Alpha Centauri."],["Names","Alpha Centauri A is also called Rigil Kentaurus, from an Arabic phrase meaning foot of the centaur. Alpha Centauri B is sometimes called Toliman. The International Astronomical Union officially approved these names in 2016 and 2018."]],
sp:["A pinhole in the sky","In 1915 the Scottish-born astronomer Robert Innes, director of the Union Observatory in Johannesburg, South Africa, was comparing photographs of the sky taken years apart, looking for stars that had moved. He found a faint red star near Alpha Centauri that was moving across the sky at the same rate as its bright neighbors, suggesting that it belonged to the same system. Further measurements showed it was even closer to the Sun than Alpha Centauri A and B. Innes named it Proxima Centauri, from the Latin for nearest. More than a century later, astronomers found a planet orbiting it, making our nearest stellar neighbor even more intriguing."],
g:[["Light-year","The distance light travels in one year"],["Red dwarf","A small, cool, dim star"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist"],["Parallax","The apparent shift of a star used to measure distance"]],
f:["Alpha Centauri is the nearest star system.","Proxima Centauri is the closest single star.","Proxima b is an Earth-sized planet."],
fun:"Light from Proxima Centauri takes about four years and three months to reach Earth."});

ADDART({id:"sirius",t:"Sirius",c:"Astronomy",i:"⭐",w:"Sirius",r:["stars","whitedwarfs","constellations","egypt","binarystars"],
p:[["Brightest star","Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky."],["The Dog Star","It is in the constellation Canis Major, the Great Dog."],["A hidden companion","Sirius has a tiny white dwarf partner."]],
l:"Sirius is the brightest star in Earth’s night sky, shining in the constellation Canis Major, the Great Dog, which is why it is often called the Dog Star. It appears so bright partly because it is close, only about 8.6 light-years away, and partly because it is about 25 times more luminous than the Sun. Sirius is actually a binary system: the main star, Sirius A, is a hot, white star about twice the Sun’s mass, and its faint companion, Sirius B, is a white dwarf, the dense remnant of a dead star. Sirius has been important to many cultures. In ancient Egypt, its first appearance before dawn signaled the coming flood of the Nile. From the Philippines, it shines brilliantly in the evening sky from about December to April.",
s:[["Sirius A","Sirius A is a white, main-sequence star hotter than the Sun, with a surface temperature of about 9,700 degrees Celsius. It is about twice as massive and nearly twice as wide as the Sun. Because of its brightness and position, it often twinkles strongly in many colors when low in the sky."],["Sirius B","Sirius B is a white dwarf about the size of Earth but with roughly the mass of the Sun. It orbits Sirius A every 50 years. It is about 10,000 times fainter than Sirius A, making it hard to see through telescopes because of the main star’s glare. It was the first white dwarf to be discovered."],["Finding Sirius","Sirius is easy to find by following the three stars of Orion’s Belt downward to the left in the evening sky. It shines with a brilliant blue-white light. Only the Moon and the planets Venus and Jupiter, and sometimes Mars, appear brighter in the night sky."],["Moving star","Sirius is moving through space relative to the Sun. In 1718 Edmond Halley noticed that Sirius, Arcturus and Aldebaran had shifted from positions recorded by ancient Greek astronomers, the first evidence that stars move. Sirius is slowly getting closer to us and will grow slightly brighter over the next tens of thousands of years."]],
k:[["Constellation","Canis Major"],["Distance","About 8.6 light-years"],["Brightness","Brightest star in the night sky"],["Companion","Sirius B, a white dwarf"],["Nickname","The Dog Star"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Ancient Egyptians link Sirius’s rising with the Nile flood"],["1718","Edmond Halley discovers that Sirius moves across the sky"],["1844","Friedrich Bessel predicts an unseen companion"],["1862","Alvan Graham Clark sees Sirius B"],["1915","Walter Adams shows Sirius B is very hot and dense"],["2005","Hubble measures Sirius B’s mass precisely"]],
s2:[["Sirius in ancient Egypt","The ancient Egyptians called Sirius Sopdet. After being hidden in the Sun’s glare for about 70 days each year, it reappeared in the eastern sky just before sunrise in midsummer, around the time the Nile began to flood. This heliacal rising marked the start of the Egyptian new year and the farming cycle, and Sopdet was worshipped as a goddess."],["Dog days","The ancient Greeks and Romans noticed that Sirius rose with the Sun during the hottest part of summer and believed its heat added to the Sun’s. They called this period the dog days, after the Dog Star. The phrase is still used in English for the hottest days of summer, although the timing of Sirius’s rising has shifted over the centuries."],["Navigation and calendars","Sirius was used by Polynesian navigators to find their way across the Pacific, as it passes almost directly over Fiji. Many other peoples used its rising and setting to mark seasons. In parts of Southeast Asia, bright stars like Sirius and the Pleiades helped farmers decide when to plant."],["The Dogon controversy","In the 1930s and 1940s, French anthropologists Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen reported that the Dogon people of Mali had detailed traditional knowledge of Sirius B, which is invisible to the naked eye. Some writers claimed this proved ancient contact with aliens. Later researchers have questioned the anthropologists’ methods and suggest that any such knowledge may have come from contact with Europeans, or was misunderstood. The debate shows how careful scholars must be in interpreting cultural information."],["Was Sirius red?","Some ancient texts, including writings by the Roman astronomer Ptolemy, appear to describe Sirius as reddish. This puzzled astronomers, since Sirius is clearly white today and stars do not change color so quickly. Most scientists believe the descriptions refer to its strong twinkling when low on the horizon or are mistranslations."],["Sirius from the Philippines","From the Philippines, Sirius rises in the east in the evening around December and is high in the sky by February and March, shining as the brightest star in the night. Stargazers often see it twinkle in rainbow colors when it is close to the horizon, an effect caused by Earth’s atmosphere."]],
sp:["The star that wobbled","In the 1830s and 1840s, the German astronomer Friedrich Bessel carefully measured the position of Sirius over many years. He noticed that, instead of moving in a straight line across the sky, Sirius followed a slightly wavy path. In 1844 he concluded that an unseen companion must be orbiting it, pulling it back and forth. For nearly two decades, no one could see the companion. Then, in 1862, Alvan Graham Clark, testing a new 18.5-inch telescope lens made by his father’s company in Massachusetts, pointed it at Sirius and spotted a faint point of light right beside it. It was Sirius B, later shown to be a white dwarf, a new and strange type of star."],
g:[["Binary star","Two stars orbiting each other"],["White dwarf","The small, dense remains of a star"],["Heliacal rising","A star’s first appearance before dawn after being hidden"],["Luminosity","The total light a star gives off"]],
f:["Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky.","It is about 8.6 light-years away.","Sirius B is a white dwarf."],
fun:"The phrase dog days comes from Sirius, the Dog Star."});

;
/* data/space14.js */
// Space: Polaris, the Crab Nebula, the Parker Solar Probe, Juno and the Mir space station.
ADDART({id:"polaris",t:"Polaris, the North Star",c:"Astronomy",i:"⭐",w:"Polaris",r:["stars","constellations","maps","sirius","binarystars"],
p:[["Fixed in the north","Polaris sits almost directly above Earth’s North Pole."],["Guide for travelers","Sailors and travelers have used it to find north for centuries."],["Not forever","Because Earth wobbles, other stars will become the North Star."]],
l:"Polaris, also called the North Star or Pole Star, is a star in the constellation Ursa Minor, the Little Bear, that lies very close to the point in the sky directly above Earth’s North Pole. Because of this, it appears almost motionless while other stars circle around it through the night, making it a reliable guide for finding north. Its height above the horizon roughly equals the observer’s latitude, which sailors used for navigation. Polaris is not the brightest star in the sky, but it is easy to find using the pointer stars of the Big Dipper. It is a yellow supergiant about 430 light-years away and part of a triple star system. Because Earth’s axis slowly wobbles, the role of North Star passes between different stars over thousands of years.",
s:[["Finding Polaris","Polaris can be found by following the two stars at the end of the bowl of the Big Dipper, in Ursa Major, upward about five times the distance between them. It is the last star in the handle of the Little Dipper. From the Philippines, Polaris appears low in the northern sky."],["Latitude from a star","Because Polaris sits near the celestial pole, its angle above the horizon tells an observer’s latitude. At the North Pole, it is directly overhead; at the equator, it sits on the horizon. In Manila, at about 14.6 degrees north, Polaris appears about 15 degrees above the northern horizon, while in Mindanao it is lower still."],["A pulsing supergiant","Polaris is a yellow supergiant, much larger and brighter than the Sun. It is a Cepheid variable star, which pulses in brightness over about four days, though the changes are small. Polaris has two smaller companion stars; one, Polaris Ab, orbits very close and was first directly imaged by the Hubble Space Telescope."],["Changing pole stars","Earth’s axis slowly traces a circle in the sky over about 26,000 years, a motion called precession. Around 2700 BC, when the Egyptian pyramids were built, the star Thuban was the pole star. Polaris will be closest to the pole around the year 2100, and in about 12,000 years the bright star Vega will be near the pole."]],
k:[["Constellation","Ursa Minor"],["Distance","About 430 light-years"],["Type","Yellow supergiant, Cepheid variable"],["Closest to the pole","Around 2100"],["Future pole star","Vega, in about 12,000 years"]],
d:[["c. 2700 BC","Thuban serves as the pole star"],["c. 1000 AD","Polaris is near enough to the pole to guide navigators"],["1780","William Herschel discovers a companion star of Polaris"],["1911","Ejnar Hertzsprung confirms that Polaris varies in brightness"],["2006","Hubble images Polaris’s close companion"],["2100","Polaris will come closest to the celestial pole"]],
s2:[["Navigation at sea","For centuries, sailors in the Northern Hemisphere measured the height of Polaris with instruments such as the astrolabe, quadrant and sextant to determine their latitude. Combined with other methods, this helped navigators cross oceans. In the Southern Hemisphere, where Polaris cannot be seen, sailors used other stars such as the Southern Cross."],["Following the drinking gourd","In the United States before the Civil War, enslaved people escaping to freedom in the North were said to follow the drinking gourd, a name for the Big Dipper, which points to the North Star. The North Star became a symbol of freedom, and the abolitionist Frederick Douglass named his newspaper The North Star."],["Polaris and the Philippines","From the Philippines, close to the equator, Polaris sits low in the northern sky and can be hidden by trees, buildings or haze. Traditional Filipino seafarers relied on many stars, the Sun, winds and currents to navigate, rather than on Polaris alone. Today, astronomy groups teach how to find Polaris during stargazing events."],["Not the brightest","A common misconception is that the North Star is the brightest star in the sky. In fact, Polaris is only around the 48th brightest. Sirius is the brightest. Polaris is important not because of its brightness but because of its position."],["Polaris in culture","Many cultures gave Polaris special meaning. In Chinese astronomy, the region around the pole represented the emperor’s palace. In some Norse and Siberian traditions, the North Star was the nail or post holding up the sky. It appears often in poetry and song as a symbol of constancy."],["A star of uncertain distance","Measuring the distance to Polaris has proved surprisingly difficult, and estimates have varied between about 320 and 450 light-years. Because Polaris is a Cepheid variable, whose brightness is used to measure cosmic distances, getting its distance right matters for calibrating the cosmic distance scale."]],
sp:["Shakespeare’s constant star","In William Shakespeare’s play Julius Caesar, Caesar declares that he is as constant as the northern star, which has no equal in the heavens for being fixed and steady. Shakespeare’s audience knew that Polaris seemed to stay still while the other stars wheeled around it. But in Caesar’s own time, around 44 BC, Polaris was actually farther from the pole than it is today, and no bright star marked the pole exactly. Astronomers enjoy this detail because it shows how precession slowly changes the sky. The North Star that seems so constant over a human lifetime is, over thousands of years, just one of many stars to take its turn."],
g:[["Pole star","A star near the celestial pole"],["Celestial pole","The point in the sky above Earth’s pole"],["Precession","The slow wobble of Earth’s axis"],["Latitude","Distance north or south of the equator"]],
f:["Polaris is near the north celestial pole.","It is not the brightest star.","Vega will become the North Star in the future."],
fun:"When the Egyptian pyramids were built, a star called Thuban was the North Star, not Polaris."});

ADDART({id:"crabnebula",t:"The Crab Nebula",c:"Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Crab Nebula",r:["supernova","pulsar","nebula","stars","hubblest"],
p:[["Remains of an explosion","The Crab Nebula is the remnant of a star that exploded in 1054."],["Recorded in history","Chinese astronomers recorded the explosion as a guest star."],["A spinning heart","At its center is a pulsar spinning 30 times a second."]],
l:"The Crab Nebula is the glowing remains of a supernova, the explosion of a massive star, located about 6,500 light-years away in the constellation Taurus. The explosion was seen from Earth in the year 1054 and recorded by Chinese and other astronomers as a guest star bright enough to be visible in daylight for weeks. The expanding cloud of gas, about 11 light-years across, contains colorful filaments and glows across the spectrum from radio waves to gamma rays. At its center lies the Crab Pulsar, a rapidly spinning neutron star, the collapsed core of the original star, which rotates about 30 times per second and powers much of the nebula’s light. It is one of the most studied objects in astronomy and the first entry in Charles Messier’s catalog, M1.",
s:[["The guest star of 1054","Chinese court astronomers of the Song dynasty recorded a new star appearing in July 1054 near the constellation we call Taurus. It was visible in daylight for about 23 days and at night for nearly two years. Japanese and Middle Eastern records also mention it. Modern astronomers linked these records to the Crab Nebula by tracing its expansion back in time."],["Filaments and glow","The nebula has a web of red and orange filaments made of hydrogen and other elements thrown off by the dying star. Inside, a bluish haze comes from electrons spiraling at nearly the speed of light in strong magnetic fields, producing what is called synchrotron radiation."],["The Crab Pulsar","In 1968 astronomers discovered that a faint star at the nebula’s center was flashing in radio waves about 30 times a second. It was a pulsar, a neutron star only about 20 kilometers wide but with more mass than the Sun. Its discovery confirmed that neutron stars form in supernova explosions."],["A cosmic laboratory","Because it is bright and well studied, the Crab Nebula is used to calibrate X-ray and gamma-ray telescopes. Scientists study how its pulsar wind shapes the nebula, how particles are accelerated to enormous energies and how the cloud changes over time."]],
k:[["Constellation","Taurus"],["Distance","About 6,500 light-years"],["Supernova seen","1054"],["Central object","Crab Pulsar"],["Catalog name","Messier 1"]],
d:[["1054","Astronomers in China record a guest star"],["1731","John Bevis first observes the nebula through a telescope"],["1758","Charles Messier observes it, later listing it as M1"],["1844","Lord Rosse’s drawing leads to the name Crab Nebula"],["1968","The Crab Pulsar is discovered"],["2023","The James Webb Space Telescope releases a detailed image of the nebula"]],
s2:[["Messier’s first object","In 1758 the French astronomer Charles Messier was searching for a returning comet when he found the Crab Nebula. Realizing that fuzzy objects like this could be confused with comets, he began a list of such objects to avoid, placing the Crab first. The Messier catalog, which includes many famous nebulae and galaxies, is still widely used by amateur astronomers."],["How it got its name","In 1844 the Irish astronomer William Parsons, the Earl of Rosse, observed the nebula with his large telescope and drew it with filaments that reminded him of a crab’s legs. The name Crab Nebula stuck, even though later images look less like a crab."],["Ancient rock art?","Some researchers have suggested that a rock painting at Chaco Canyon in New Mexico, showing a crescent, a star and a hand, may depict the 1054 supernova next to the Moon, as seen by ancestral Puebloan people. Others argue the image could have different meanings. The idea remains debated."],["Expanding cloud","Comparing photographs taken years apart shows that the Crab Nebula is expanding at about 1,500 kilometers per second. Tracing this expansion backward points to an explosion around the 11th century, matching the historical records."],["Images from space telescopes","The Hubble Space Telescope produced a famous detailed mosaic of the Crab Nebula in 2005. In 2023 the James Webb Space Telescope captured its filaments and dust in infrared light, helping scientists understand what kind of supernova created it."],["Seeing the Crab","The Crab Nebula can be seen as a faint, fuzzy patch through a small telescope near the star Zeta Tauri, at the tip of one of the bull’s horns in Taurus. From the Philippines, it is well placed in the evening sky from about December to March, near the constellation Orion."]],
sp:["The guest star","On July 4, 1054, an official astronomer at the Song dynasty court in China noticed a brilliant new star in the eastern sky before dawn, near a star in what we now call Taurus. He recorded it as a guest star and reported its brightness and color. For 23 days, it was visible even in daylight, as bright as Venus. Then it slowly faded, finally disappearing from view after about two years. Nearly 900 years later, astronomers measured the expansion of the Crab Nebula and calculated that it began expanding about nine centuries earlier. Matching this with the Chinese records, they realized the guest star had been the explosion that created the nebula."],
g:[["Supernova remnant","The cloud left after a star explodes"],["Pulsar","A rapidly spinning neutron star that emits beams"],["Guest star","An old Chinese term for a new star"],["Synchrotron radiation","Light given off by fast electrons in magnetic fields"]],
f:["The Crab Nebula formed in a supernova seen in 1054.","Its pulsar spins about 30 times a second.","It is the first object in Messier’s catalog."],
fun:"The supernova that formed the Crab Nebula was visible in daylight for over three weeks."});

ADDART({id:"parkerprobe",t:"The Parker Solar Probe",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☀️",w:"Parker Solar Probe",r:["sun","spaceweather","aurora","voyager","rocket"],
p:[["Touching the Sun","The Parker Solar Probe flies through the Sun’s outer atmosphere."],["Fastest object","It is the fastest object ever built by humans."],["Heat shield","A thick shield protects it from intense heat."]],
l:"The Parker Solar Probe is a NASA spacecraft launched in August 2018 to study the Sun up close. It is named after Eugene Parker, the American physicist who predicted the solar wind in 1958, and was the first NASA spacecraft named after a living person. Using repeated flybys of Venus to tighten its orbit, the probe has flown closer to the Sun than any other spacecraft, passing through the Sun’s outer atmosphere, the corona. In December 2024 it passed about 6.1 million kilometers from the Sun’s surface, traveling at about 690,000 kilometers per hour, making it the fastest human-made object. A carbon-composite heat shield keeps its instruments near room temperature while the front faces temperatures around 1,000 degrees Celsius or more.",
s:[["Why go so close?","Scientists want to understand two puzzles: why the Sun’s corona is hundreds of times hotter than its surface, and how the solar wind is accelerated to high speeds. These questions matter because the solar wind and solar storms affect satellites, communications, power grids and astronauts."],["Using Venus","Getting close to the Sun is surprisingly hard, because a spacecraft launched from Earth carries Earth’s sideways speed around the Sun. Parker used seven flybys of Venus to gradually slow its orbital motion and bring its closest point nearer and nearer to the Sun."],["The heat shield","The probe’s Thermal Protection System is about 11.4 centimeters thick, made of carbon foam sandwiched between carbon-composite sheets, with a white coating that reflects sunlight. Behind it, the instruments stay at around 30 degrees Celsius. The probe must keep the shield pointed precisely at the Sun, adjusting itself automatically."],["Touching the corona","In April 2021 Parker crossed into the corona for the first time, an event NASA described as touching the Sun. Its instruments sampled particles and magnetic fields there directly, something no previous spacecraft had done."]],
k:[["Launched","August 12, 2018"],["Named after","Eugene Parker"],["Closest approach","About 6.1 million km, December 2024"],["Top speed","About 690,000 km/h"],["Shield thickness","About 11.4 cm"]],
d:[["1958","Eugene Parker predicts the solar wind"],["2018","The Parker Solar Probe is launched"],["2021","The probe enters the Sun’s corona for the first time"],["2022","Eugene Parker dies at age 94"],["2024","The probe makes its record closest approach to the Sun"],["2025","Scientists release close-up images from inside the corona"]],
s2:[["Eugene Parker","In 1958 Eugene Parker, a young physicist at the University of Chicago, proposed that the Sun’s hot corona must continually expand outward as a supersonic wind of charged particles. Many scientists rejected the idea, and his paper was nearly rejected by reviewers. Within a few years, spacecraft confirmed the solar wind. In 2018, at age 91, Parker watched the launch of the probe named after him. He died in 2022."],["Switchbacks","One of Parker’s surprising discoveries was switchbacks, sudden S-shaped kinks in the Sun’s magnetic field carried by the solar wind. Scientists have traced some of them to the Sun’s surface, where they may help heat and accelerate the solar wind."],["Images from inside","Parker’s camera, called WISPR, has taken images from within the corona, showing streamers, the solar wind flowing outward and dust around the Sun. Images released in 2025 from its record close pass showed structures in the solar wind in unprecedented detail."],["Venus surprises","During its flybys of Venus, Parker’s camera unexpectedly captured images of the planet’s surface glowing through its thick clouds at night in near-infrared light, revealing continents and plains. This bonus science helped researchers study Venus’s surface."],["Space weather","Solar storms can disrupt GPS, radio communications and power grids and damage satellites. Data from Parker help scientists improve forecasts of space weather. In the Philippines, the weather service PAGASA and the Philippine Space Agency monitor space weather alerts from international partners."],["A memory card for the Sun","Parker carries a memory card containing the names of more than a million people who signed up to send their names to the Sun, as well as photos of Eugene Parker and a copy of his 1958 paper. It is one of many ways space agencies involve the public in missions."]],
sp:["A wind no one believed in","In the 1950s, most scientists thought space between the planets was calm and empty. Eugene Parker, studying how comets’ tails always point away from the Sun, calculated that the Sun’s extremely hot corona could not stay still; it must flow outward constantly as a solar wind. When he submitted his paper in 1958, two reviewers rejected it. The journal’s editor, the astrophysicist Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, could not find a mistake in Parker’s math and published it anyway. In 1962 NASA’s Mariner 2 spacecraft, on its way to Venus, measured the solar wind exactly as Parker predicted. Six decades later, the spacecraft carrying his name flew into the corona itself."],
g:[["Corona","The Sun’s outer atmosphere"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s path"],["Space weather","Conditions in space caused by the Sun"]],
f:["The Parker Solar Probe has entered the Sun’s corona.","It is the fastest human-made object.","It is named after Eugene Parker."],
fun:"At its top speed, the Parker Solar Probe could travel from Manila to Davao in about five seconds."});

ADDART({id:"junoprobe",t:"The Juno Mission",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Juno (spacecraft)",r:["jupiter","io","ganymede","europa","cassini"],
p:[["Orbiting Jupiter","Juno has orbited Jupiter since 2016."],["Solar powered","It was the first solar-powered spacecraft to reach Jupiter."],["Storms at the poles","Juno revealed giant clusters of cyclones at Jupiter’s poles."]],
l:"Juno is a NASA spacecraft that has been orbiting Jupiter since July 2016, studying the giant planet’s interior, atmosphere, magnetic field and moons. Launched in August 2011, it was the first spacecraft to reach Jupiter powered by solar panels instead of nuclear generators, using three large solar arrays. Juno travels in a long, looping orbit that passes over Jupiter’s poles, allowing it to see regions never observed before. It discovered clusters of giant cyclones at both poles, showed that Jupiter’s Great Red Spot extends hundreds of kilometers deep, and found that the planet’s core is larger and fuzzier than expected. In an extended mission, Juno also made close flybys of the moons Ganymede, Europa and Io. Its JunoCam images are processed by members of the public.",
s:[["Mission goals","Juno’s main goals are to learn how Jupiter formed and evolved, by measuring how much water its atmosphere contains, mapping its gravity and magnetic fields to probe its interior, and studying its powerful auroras. Understanding Jupiter helps scientists understand how the whole solar system formed."],["Polar orbit","Unlike earlier missions, Juno orbits over Jupiter’s north and south poles, swooping close to the cloud tops and then swinging far away to escape the planet’s intense radiation. Its sensitive electronics are protected inside a titanium vault."],["Cyclones at the poles","Juno found that Jupiter’s north pole is surrounded by eight cyclones arranged around a central one, and the south pole by five or six, each as wide as a continent. These geometric patterns have remained stable for years, puzzling scientists."],["Inside Jupiter","Gravity measurements showed that Jupiter’s core is not small and solid, but large and diluted, mixed with surrounding layers. This suggests that Jupiter formed in a more complex way than once thought, possibly involving a giant collision early in its history."]],
k:[["Launched","August 5, 2011"],["Arrived at Jupiter","July 4, 2016"],["Power","Solar panels"],["Orbit","Over Jupiter’s poles"],["Moons visited","Ganymede, Europa, Io"]],
d:[["2011","Juno is launched from Florida"],["2013","Juno flies past Earth for a gravity boost"],["2016","Juno enters orbit around Jupiter"],["2021","Juno flies past Ganymede"],["2022","Juno flies past Europa"],["2023","Juno begins close flybys of Io"]],
s2:[["The name","In Roman mythology, the god Jupiter drew a veil of clouds around himself to hide his mischief, and only his wife, the goddess Juno, could see through the clouds to his true nature. NASA named the spacecraft Juno because it was designed to peer beneath Jupiter’s clouds."],["Lego passengers","Juno carries three small aluminum figures made in the style of Lego toys, representing the god Jupiter, the goddess Juno and the astronomer Galileo Galilei, who discovered Jupiter’s four largest moons. It also carries a plaque honoring Galileo, with a portrait and his handwriting."],["Citizen scientists","JunoCam, the spacecraft’s color camera, was included mainly for public engagement. NASA posts raw images online, and amateur image processors around the world turn them into stunning pictures of Jupiter’s swirling clouds. Some of these citizen-processed images have been used in scientific studies."],["The Great Red Spot","Juno measured the depth of Jupiter’s Great Red Spot, a storm larger than Earth that has existed for centuries. In 2021 scientists reported that the storm extends roughly 300 to 500 kilometers deep, much deeper than Earth’s oceans, though shallower than some had expected."],["Visiting the moons","In its extended mission, Juno flew close past Ganymede in 2021 and Europa in 2022, taking the best images of them in decades. In late 2023 and early 2024, it made two very close flybys of Io, revealing lava lakes, mountains and volcanic plumes on the most volcanic world in the solar system."],["Solar power far from the Sun","Jupiter receives only about 4 percent as much sunlight as Earth. To generate enough power, Juno has three solar arrays, each about 9 meters long, giving it a total span of about 20 meters. When Juno reached Jupiter, no solar-powered spacecraft had ever traveled so far from the Sun."]],
sp:["Arrival on the Fourth of July","On July 4, 2016, scientists and engineers gathered at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California, anxiously watching data on their screens. Nearly five years after launch, Juno was approaching Jupiter at enormous speed and needed to fire its main engine for 35 minutes to slow down enough to be captured into orbit. Because radio signals took 48 minutes to reach Earth, the engine burn was controlled automatically; the team could only wait. Finally, tones from the spacecraft confirmed that the burn had worked and Juno was in orbit. The control room erupted in applause. In the following years, Juno would send back some of the most detailed views of Jupiter ever seen."],
g:[["Polar orbit","An orbit passing over a planet’s poles"],["Solar array","A panel of solar cells"],["Cyclone","A large rotating storm"],["Extended mission","Additional work after a mission’s main goals"]],
f:["Juno has orbited Jupiter since 2016.","It is powered by solar panels.","It carries three Lego-style figures."],
fun:"Juno carries three tiny Lego-style figures of Jupiter, Juno and Galileo."});

ADDART({id:"mir",t:"The Mir Space Station",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛰️",w:"Mir",r:["iss","astronauts","space","spaceshuttle","tiangong"],
p:[["The first modular station","Mir was built piece by piece in orbit."],["Fifteen years in space","It orbited Earth from 1986 to 2001."],["Records and crises","Mir set endurance records but also survived a fire and a collision."]],
l:"Mir, meaning peace or world in Russian, was a space station operated by the Soviet Union and later Russia from 1986 to 2001. It was the first space station assembled from several modules launched separately and joined in orbit, a design later used for the International Space Station. Mir was occupied almost continuously for nearly ten years and hosted astronauts from many countries, including the United States, Japan, France, Germany and Britain. On Mir, Russian cosmonaut Valeri Polyakov set the record for the longest single spaceflight, about 437 days. The station also suffered serious emergencies, including a fire and a collision with a supply ship in 1997. After 15 years in orbit, it was deliberately brought down into the South Pacific Ocean in 2001.",
s:[["Building Mir","The core module of Mir was launched on February 20, 1986. Over the following ten years, six more modules were added, including laboratories for science, astronomy, materials research and Earth observation. A docking module allowed American space shuttles to connect to the station."],["Life on Mir","Crews of two or three people usually lived on Mir for months at a time, carrying out experiments in biology, medicine, physics and astronomy. They exercised daily to reduce bone and muscle loss, grew plants and studied how long-duration spaceflight affects the human body, knowledge later used for the International Space Station."],["International cooperation","After the Cold War, Russia and the United States worked together on the Shuttle–Mir program from 1995 to 1998. American space shuttles docked with Mir nine times, and seven American astronauts lived aboard for long stays. This cooperation helped prepare for building the International Space Station."],["The end of Mir","By the late 1990s, Mir was aging, and Russia was focusing on the International Space Station. On March 23, 2001, controllers fired Mir’s engines to lower its orbit. The station broke apart as it reentered the atmosphere, and surviving pieces fell into a remote area of the South Pacific, watched by observers in Fiji."]],
k:[["Operated by","Soviet Union, then Russia"],["In orbit","1986–2001"],["Record flight","Valeri Polyakov, about 437 days"],["Modules","Core plus six added modules"],["Fate","Deorbited into the South Pacific"]],
d:[["1986","Mir’s core module is launched"],["1991","Sergei Krikalev remains on Mir as the Soviet Union collapses"],["1995","Valeri Polyakov completes his record 437-day flight"],["1995","The Space Shuttle Atlantis docks with Mir"],["1997","Mir suffers a fire and a collision with a supply ship"],["2001","Mir is deorbited into the Pacific Ocean"]],
s2:[["The last Soviet citizen","Cosmonaut Sergei Krikalev arrived on Mir in May 1991, expecting to return in a few months. While he was in orbit, the Soviet Union collapsed in December 1991, and the country that launched him no longer existed. Because of funding problems, his return was delayed, and he spent about 311 days in space before landing in March 1992 in a new country, Russia. He was sometimes called the last Soviet citizen."],["Fire in orbit","In February 1997, an oxygen-generating canister caught fire aboard Mir, filling the station with smoke while six people were aboard, including an American astronaut. The crew put out the fire with extinguishers and wore masks until the air cleared. It was one of the most serious fires ever to occur on a spacecraft."],["The collision","In June 1997 an uncrewed Progress supply ship, being manually docked as a test, crashed into the Spektr module, puncturing its hull. Air began leaking, and the crew quickly cut cables and sealed off the module to save the rest of the station. Spektr remained sealed for the rest of Mir’s life, and the station lost some of its power."],["The longest flight","Physician cosmonaut Valeri Polyakov launched to Mir in January 1994 and returned in March 1995, after about 437 days in space, still the record for a single spaceflight. He studied how his own body adapted, and his health after landing showed that humans could survive journeys as long as a trip to Mars."],["Visitors from many nations","Mir hosted astronauts from many countries, including Syria, Afghanistan, Bulgaria, France, Germany, Austria, Japan, Britain, Slovakia and the United States. Helen Sharman became the first British astronaut when she flew to Mir in 1991. A Japanese television journalist, Toyohiro Akiyama, visited in 1990 as one of the first people to fly into space on a commercial basis."],["Mir’s legacy","Mir showed that people could live and work in space for long periods, tested modular construction and developed ways to resupply stations with cargo ships. Some of its design ideas continued in the Russian segment of the International Space Station, whose Zvezda service module was based on a backup Mir core module."]],
sp:["Collision in orbit","On June 25, 1997, the crew of Mir was testing a new manual docking system using an uncrewed Progress supply ship controlled from inside the station. Commander Vasily Tsibliyev watched a video screen as the cargo ship approached, but it moved too fast and he could not stop it. The Progress slammed into the Spektr module, punching a hole in its hull. Alarms sounded, and air began hissing out. Cosmonaut Alexander Lazutkin and American astronaut Michael Foale rushed to cut cables running through the hatch so it could be closed. They sealed Spektr off, saving the station. In the days that followed, the crew worked in a darkened, damaged station to restore power."],
g:[["Space station","A spacecraft where people live for long periods"],["Module","A separate section of a station"],["Docking","Joining two spacecraft in orbit"],["Deorbit","Lowering a spacecraft out of orbit"]],
f:["Mir was the first modular space station.","Valeri Polyakov spent about 437 days on Mir.","Mir fell into the Pacific Ocean in 2001."],
fun:"A cosmonaut on Mir left the Soviet Union and came home to Russia, because the country changed while he was in space."});

;
