ADDART({id:"vaping",t:"Vaping and E-cigarettes",c:"Health",i:"💨",w:"Electronic cigarette",r:["lungs","mentalyouth","asthma","heartdisease","who"],
p:[["Electronic smoking","E-cigarettes heat a liquid into a vapor that users inhale."],["A heated debate","Experts disagree on whether vapes help smokers quit or hook new users."],["New laws","Many countries, including the Philippines, have passed laws on vaping."]],
l:"Vaping is the use of electronic cigarettes, battery-powered devices that heat a liquid, usually containing nicotine, flavorings and other chemicals, into an aerosol that is inhaled. Modern e-cigarettes were invented in China in 2003 and spread worldwide in the 2010s, available as pens, pods and disposable devices in many flavors. Supporters, including some health agencies, argue that vaping is less harmful than smoking tobacco and can help adult smokers quit. Critics, including the World Health Organization and many doctors’ groups, warn that vapes are addictive, can harm the lungs and heart, and attract young people who never smoked. Countries have responded very differently, from encouraging vaping to quit smoking to banning it outright. In the Philippines, vaping is regulated under a 2022 law.",
s:[["How e-cigarettes work","An e-cigarette contains a battery, a heating coil and a cartridge or tank of liquid, often called e-liquid or vape juice. When the user inhales or presses a button, the coil heats the liquid into an aerosol. Most e-liquids contain nicotine dissolved in propylene glycol and glycerin, with added flavors."],["Nicotine and addiction","Nicotine is highly addictive. It acts on the brain’s reward system and can be especially harmful to the developing brains of teenagers, affecting attention and learning. Some vapes deliver high levels of nicotine, and users may not realize how much they are taking in."],["Health effects","Vaping avoids the tar and many of the toxic chemicals produced by burning tobacco, which is why many scientists consider it less harmful than smoking. However, vape aerosols can contain metals and chemicals that may irritate the lungs and affect the heart and blood vessels. Because vaping is relatively new, its long-term effects are still being studied."],["Young people","Surveys in many countries have found rising vaping among teenagers, often attracted by fruity and sweet flavors, colorful designs and social media marketing. Health groups worry that vaping could lead some young people to start smoking cigarettes, while others argue that most teens who vape would otherwise have smoked."]],
k:[["Invented","2003, China"],["Main addictive substance","Nicotine"],["Philippine law","Republic Act 11900, 2022"],["Main debate","Quitting aid versus new addiction"],["Related outbreak","EVALI, United States, 2019"]],
d:[["2003","Pharmacist Hon Lik invents the modern e-cigarette in China"],["2010s","Vaping spreads rapidly worldwide"],["2019","A lung injury outbreak linked to vaping products strikes the United States"],["2022","The Philippines passes a law regulating vape products"],["2024","Australia restricts vapes to pharmacies"],["2025","The United Kingdom bans disposable vapes"]],
s2:[["The Philippine vape law","In 2022 Republic Act 11900, the Vaporized Nicotine and Non-Nicotine Products Regulation Act, lapsed into law. It regulates the sale, packaging and advertising of vapes and heated tobacco products and set the minimum age for buying them at 18. Supporters said it would bring the growing industry under clear rules. Health groups and some lawmakers opposed it, arguing that the age limit was too low and that oversight should be with health authorities rather than the trade department."],["A tool to quit?","In the United Kingdom, the National Health Service has encouraged adult smokers to switch to vaping as a way to quit, citing studies showing that e-cigarettes can be more effective than nicotine patches or gum for some people. Other experts caution that many people end up using both cigarettes and vapes, or remain addicted to nicotine for years."],["Different approaches","Countries have chosen very different policies. Some, such as India, Thailand and Singapore, ban the sale of e-cigarettes. Others, such as the United Kingdom and New Zealand, allow them with restrictions to help smokers quit, while tightening rules to protect young people. Australia made most vapes available only through pharmacies in 2024."],["EVALI","In 2019 the United States experienced an outbreak of serious lung injuries among people who vaped, called EVALI, which led to many hospitalizations and dozens of deaths. Investigators linked most cases to vitamin E acetate, an additive found mainly in illegal vape products containing THC, a substance from cannabis, rather than in regular nicotine vapes."],["Disposable vapes and waste","Disposable vapes, which contain lithium batteries and plastic, are thrown away after use, creating electronic waste and fire risks in garbage trucks and dumps. Concerns about waste and their appeal to young people led the United Kingdom to ban disposable vapes in 2025, and other countries are considering similar rules."],["Heated tobacco","Heated tobacco products are different from e-cigarettes: they heat real tobacco without burning it. Tobacco companies market them as less harmful alternatives. Independent researchers and health agencies continue to study how much risk they reduce compared with cigarettes."]],
sp:["A pharmacist’s invention","In the early 2000s, Hon Lik, a pharmacist in Beijing, was a heavy smoker whose father had died of lung cancer. He wanted a way to get nicotine without smoke. Inspired, he later said, by a dream, he developed a device that used ultrasound and later a heating element to turn a nicotine solution into vapor. He patented it in 2003, and the Chinese company he worked for began selling it in 2004. His invention spread around the world and grew into a huge industry. Hon Lik himself has said he hoped it would help smokers like him, but his invention has also become one of the most debated health products of the 21st century."],
g:[["E-cigarette","A device that heats liquid into an inhaled aerosol"],["Nicotine","An addictive chemical found in tobacco"],["Aerosol","A mist of tiny particles in air"],["Harm reduction","Reducing harm from risky behavior without requiring complete stopping"]],
f:["Modern e-cigarettes were invented in China in 2003.","Most vapes contain addictive nicotine.","Countries have very different vaping laws."],
fun:"The inventor of the modern e-cigarette said the idea came to him partly in a dream."});

ADDART({id:"carbonfootprint",t:"Carbon Footprints and Net Zero",c:"Environment",i:"👣",w:"Carbon footprint",r:["climchange","energytransition","fossilfuels","renew","sdgs","climateactivism"],
p:[["Measuring emissions","A carbon footprint measures the greenhouse gases caused by a person, product or country."],["Net zero","Net zero means balancing emissions with removals."],["Unequal footprints","Footprints differ hugely between rich and poor countries."]],
l:"A carbon footprint is the total amount of greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide and methane, released into the atmosphere as a result of the activities of a person, organization, product, event or country. It is usually measured in metric tons of carbon dioxide equivalent. Carbon footprints include emissions from burning fossil fuels for electricity, transport and heating, from making goods and food, and from cutting down forests. Net zero is a goal of reducing emissions as much as possible and balancing any that remain by removing the same amount from the air, for example by planting forests. Under the 2015 Paris Agreement, countries aim to limit global warming, and many governments and companies have pledged to reach net zero by around 2050. Footprints vary enormously between countries and between rich and poor people.",
s:[["What counts","A person’s carbon footprint includes direct emissions, such as fuel burned in cars and stoves, and indirect emissions, such as those from producing the electricity, food, clothes and gadgets they use. Flying, eating large amounts of beef, and using electricity from coal power tend to have large footprints."],["Different footprints","The average person in wealthy countries such as the United States or Australia has a carbon footprint many times larger than the average person in countries such as the Philippines, India or most of Africa. The richest people in the world, who fly often and consume more, have far larger footprints than the poorest, who have contributed little to climate change."],["Net zero explained","Reaching net zero does not mean emitting nothing. It means cutting emissions deeply, by switching to renewable energy, electric vehicles and efficient buildings, and removing remaining emissions through forests, soil and technologies that capture carbon. Scientists say global carbon dioxide emissions must reach net zero around mid-century to limit warming to about 1.5 degrees Celsius."],["Carbon offsets","Some people and companies buy carbon offsets, paying for projects that plant trees, protect forests or reduce emissions elsewhere to balance their own emissions. Supporters say offsets direct money to useful projects. Critics argue that many offset projects exaggerate their benefits or would have happened anyway, and that offsets can be used to avoid real emission cuts."]],
k:[["Unit","Metric tons of CO₂ equivalent"],["Main global agreement","Paris Agreement, 2015"],["Common net-zero target","Around 2050"],["Large personal sources","Flying, driving, meat, electricity"],["Philippine pledge","75% reduction by 2030, mostly conditional"]],
d:[["1992","The UN Framework Convention on Climate Change is adopted"],["1997","The Kyoto Protocol sets emission limits for rich countries"],["2004","An oil company advertising campaign popularizes the term carbon footprint"],["2015","The Paris Agreement is adopted"],["2018","Scientists report on limiting warming to 1.5 °C"],["2021","The Philippines submits its emission reduction pledge"]],
s2:[["Who popularized the term?","The idea of measuring ecological footprints developed in the 1990s. The phrase carbon footprint became widely known after a 2004 advertising campaign by the oil company BP that encouraged people to calculate their personal footprints. Some critics have argued that this shifted attention from fossil fuel companies to individuals, while others say personal awareness is still valuable alongside larger changes."],["The Philippines and emissions","The Philippines produces a small share of global emissions, and its per-person footprint is well below the world average. Yet it is one of the countries most vulnerable to climate change, facing stronger typhoons and rising seas. In 2021 it pledged to cut greenhouse gas emissions by 75 percent by 2030 compared with business as usual, with most of the cut conditional on international finance and technology support."],["Individual or systemic change?","People debate whether climate action should focus on individual choices or on large-scale changes by governments and industries. Supporters of personal action say choices like using public transport, eating less meat and saving electricity add up and influence others. Others argue that the biggest reductions require changing energy systems, transport and industry through policy and investment. Many experts say both are needed."],["Footprints of everyday things","Researchers calculate carbon footprints of products. For example, beef generally has a much higher footprint than rice, vegetables or beans, mainly because of land use and methane from cattle. A long-haul flight can produce more emissions per passenger than many months of driving. Locally grown food is not always lower in emissions, because the way it is produced often matters more than transport."],["Net-zero pledges","Many countries, cities and companies have announced net-zero targets. Supporters say these pledges send signals to investors and drive innovation. Critics warn that some targets are vague, rely heavily on future technology or offsets, and are not matched by short-term action. Independent groups track whether pledges are being met."],["Carbon pricing","Some governments put a price on carbon through taxes or emissions trading systems, making polluters pay for their emissions and encouraging cleaner choices. The European Union has run an emissions trading system since 2005. Supporters say carbon pricing is efficient; opponents worry about higher costs for consumers and businesses."]],
sp:["Calculating a family’s footprint","A family in Quezon City decides to estimate its carbon footprint as a school project. The children list their electricity use from the monthly bill, their jeepney and car trips, the food they eat and a vacation flight to Cebu. Using an online calculator, they find that electricity, much of it from coal power, and the flight make up a large part of their footprint. They decide to switch to energy-efficient lights, unplug appliances, take public transport more often and eat more vegetables. They also learn that their footprint is still far smaller than that of an average family in a rich country, and that national choices about energy sources matter even more than their own changes."],
g:[["Carbon footprint","The total greenhouse gases caused by an activity"],["Net zero","Balancing emissions with removals"],["Carbon offset","Paying for emission reductions elsewhere"],["CO₂ equivalent","A unit comparing different greenhouse gases"]],
f:["Carbon footprints vary greatly between countries.","Net zero balances emissions with removals.","The Paris Agreement was adopted in 2015."],
fun:"A single long-haul flight can produce more emissions per passenger than months of daily commuting by car."});

ADDART({id:"microplastics",t:"Microplastics",c:"Environment",i:"🧴",w:"Microplastics",r:["plasticwaste","sea","pollution","recycling","polymers"],
p:[["Tiny plastic pieces","Microplastics are plastic pieces smaller than 5 millimeters."],["Everywhere","They have been found from Mount Everest to the deepest ocean."],["Inside us","Scientists have found microplastics in human blood and organs."]],
l:"Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic less than 5 millimeters long, about the size of a sesame seed or smaller. Some are made small on purpose, such as microbeads once added to face scrubs and toothpaste, while most come from larger plastic items breaking down, synthetic clothing shedding fibers in the wash, car tires wearing on roads and plastic packaging fragmenting in sunlight and waves. Microplastics have been found almost everywhere scientists have looked: in oceans, rivers, soil, air, rain, Arctic ice, Mount Everest’s snow and the deepest ocean trenches. They are also found in food, drinking water and the human body. Their effects on health are still being studied. The Philippines, like many countries, faces a large plastic pollution problem, and governments are negotiating a global plastics treaty.",
s:[["Where they come from","Primary microplastics are made small, such as microbeads and plastic pellets called nurdles used to make products. Secondary microplastics form when larger items, such as bottles, bags, sachets and fishing nets, break down. Washing synthetic clothes such as polyester releases microfibers, and tire wear produces large amounts of tiny particles."],["Spreading everywhere","Microplastics travel through rivers to the sea, are carried by ocean currents and can even blow through the air and fall with rain and snow. Scientists have found them in remote mountain lakes, the Arctic and the Mariana Trench. Even smaller particles, called nanoplastics, are harder to detect."],["In living things","Plankton, fish, shellfish, seabirds and whales swallow microplastics, which can block digestion or carry harmful chemicals. Microplastics move up food chains. They have been found in seafood, salt, honey, bottled water and tap water around the world."],["In the human body","Scientists have detected microplastics in human blood, lungs, placentas, breast milk and other tissues. A 2024 study found that people with microplastics in the fatty deposits of their arteries had a higher risk of heart attack and stroke, though it did not prove the plastics caused the problems. Researchers are working to understand the health effects."]],
k:[["Size","Less than 5 mm"],["Term popularized","2004"],["Common sources","Broken-down plastic, clothing fibers, tires"],["Smaller particles","Nanoplastics"],["Philippine law","Extended Producer Responsibility Act, 2022"]],
d:[["2004","Scientist Richard Thompson’s team popularizes the term microplastics"],["2015","The United States bans plastic microbeads in rinse-off cosmetics"],["2020","Microplastics are found in snow near the summit of Mount Everest"],["2022","Microplastics are detected in human blood"],["2022","The Philippines passes an extended producer responsibility law"],["2024","Negotiations for a global plastics treaty continue without final agreement"]],
s2:[["The Philippines and plastic","The Philippines is often listed among the countries that contribute most plastic waste to the oceans, partly because many rivers flow through densely populated areas and waste collection is limited. Sachets, small single-use packets of products such as shampoo and coffee, are popular because they are affordable but are difficult to recycle. Researchers have found microplastics in Manila Bay and in fish sold in Philippine markets."],["Laws and solutions","In 2022 the Philippines passed the Extended Producer Responsibility Act, which requires large companies to recover or offset a growing share of the plastic packaging they produce. Some cities and towns ban single-use plastic bags and straws. Supporters say producers should take responsibility for their packaging, while some businesses warn of higher costs."],["A global treaty","In 2022 United Nations member states agreed to negotiate a legally binding treaty to end plastic pollution. Negotiations have been difficult. Many countries want to limit how much new plastic is produced, while some oil- and plastic-producing countries prefer to focus on waste management and recycling. Talks in 2024 and 2025 ended without a final agreement."],["Reducing microplastics","People can reduce microplastic pollution by using fewer single-use plastics, choosing natural fibers, washing synthetic clothes less often in full loads, and supporting better waste collection. Some washing machines now include filters that catch fibers. Engineers are developing ways to filter microplastics from wastewater, though removing those already in the environment is very difficult."],["Health uncertainty","Scientists stress that the health effects of microplastics on people are not yet clearly understood. Some studies in animals and cells suggest they may cause inflammation or carry harmful chemicals. Measuring microplastics in the body is also technically challenging, and some findings have been questioned because of possible contamination in laboratories. More research is underway."],["Plastic-eating discoveries","Scientists have discovered bacteria and enzymes that can break down certain plastics, such as PET used in bottles. Researchers are improving these enzymes to recycle plastic more efficiently. These discoveries are promising but not yet able to solve the global plastic problem."]],
sp:["The scientist who named microplastics","In the early 2000s, British marine biologist Richard Thompson and his students collected sediment and plankton samples from beaches and seas around Britain. Under the microscope, they found countless tiny fragments and fibers of plastic, many invisible to the naked eye. Comparing samples collected since the 1960s, they showed that the amount had increased over time. In 2004 they published a short paper in the journal Science titled Lost at Sea: Where Is All the Plastic?, using the term microplastics. The paper launched a new field of research, and two decades later, scientists around the world, including in the Philippines, study how these tiny particles affect oceans and people."],
g:[["Microplastic","A plastic piece smaller than 5 mm"],["Microbead","A tiny plastic bead once used in cosmetics"],["Nanoplastic","An extremely small plastic particle"],["Extended producer responsibility","Making producers responsible for their product waste"]],
f:["Microplastics are smaller than 5 millimeters.","They have been found in human blood.","Washing synthetic clothes releases microfibers."],
fun:"Scientists have found microplastics both in snow near the top of Mount Everest and in the Mariana Trench."});

ADDART({id:"cryptocurrency",t:"Cryptocurrency",c:"Economics",i:"🪙",w:"Cryptocurrency",r:["crypto","money","digitalpay","onlinescams","stockm","nfts"],
p:[["Digital money","Cryptocurrencies are digital currencies secured by cryptography."],["No central bank","Most are not issued by any government."],["Boom and bust","Their prices rise and fall dramatically."]],
l:"A cryptocurrency is a form of digital money that uses cryptography to secure transactions and is usually recorded on a shared public ledger called a blockchain, rather than controlled by a central bank or government. The first and best-known cryptocurrency, Bitcoin, was launched in 2009 by an anonymous person or group using the name Satoshi Nakamoto. Thousands of others have followed, including Ether, the currency of the Ethereum network, and stablecoins tied to the value of currencies such as the US dollar. Supporters see cryptocurrencies as a way to send money freely across borders and protect savings from government control. Critics point to wild price swings, scams, crime, energy use and the risk of losses. Governments, including the Philippines, have introduced regulations for crypto businesses.",
s:[["Blockchains","A blockchain is a record of transactions shared across thousands of computers. Transactions are grouped into blocks, and each block is linked to the previous one using cryptography, making it extremely hard to change past records. Because many copies exist, no single company or government controls it."],["Mining and staking","In Bitcoin, computers called miners compete to solve difficult mathematical puzzles to add new blocks, earning new bitcoins as a reward. This process, called proof of work, uses large amounts of electricity. Ethereum switched in 2022 to a method called proof of stake, in which participants lock up coins to validate transactions, cutting its energy use dramatically."],["Wallets and keys","Cryptocurrency is held in digital wallets, protected by secret codes called private keys. Whoever has the private key controls the coins. If a key is lost, the coins may be lost forever, and if it is stolen, the coins can be taken with little chance of recovery."],["Price swings","Cryptocurrency prices are extremely volatile. Bitcoin has risen from almost nothing to tens of thousands of dollars, with several crashes along the way in which prices fell by more than half. Many investors have made large gains, and many others have lost money."]],
k:[["First cryptocurrency","Bitcoin, 2009"],["Creator","Satoshi Nakamoto, identity unknown"],["Underlying technology","Blockchain"],["Philippine regulator","Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas and the SEC"],["Main risks","Volatility, scams, hacks"]],
d:[["2008","Satoshi Nakamoto publishes the Bitcoin white paper"],["2009","The Bitcoin network begins"],["2010","Someone buys two pizzas for 10,000 bitcoins"],["2015","Ethereum launches"],["2021","El Salvador makes Bitcoin legal tender"],["2022","The FTX exchange collapses"]],
s2:[["The mysterious Satoshi","In October 2008 a paper titled Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System was posted online under the name Satoshi Nakamoto. The network began in January 2009. Satoshi worked with other developers for a couple of years, then disappeared from public view around 2011. Despite many investigations and claims, Satoshi’s true identity remains unknown."],["The pizza purchase","On May 22, 2010, a programmer in Florida paid 10,000 bitcoins for two pizzas, in one of the first known purchases of goods with Bitcoin. At the time, the coins were worth about 40 US dollars. Years later, the same amount of bitcoin would be worth hundreds of millions of dollars. Bitcoin fans celebrate May 22 as Bitcoin Pizza Day."],["Crypto in the Philippines","The Philippines has a large number of cryptocurrency users. In 2021 many Filipinos played Axie Infinity, a blockchain game in which players earned tokens they could sell, especially during pandemic lockdowns. When token prices crashed and the game’s network was hacked in 2022, many lost income. The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas registers and supervises crypto service providers, and the Securities and Exchange Commission has blocked unregistered foreign exchanges."],["Supporters’ view","Supporters argue that cryptocurrencies give people control over their own money, allow cheaper and faster transfers across borders, protect against inflation and government interference, and enable new technologies such as decentralized finance. Some see Bitcoin as digital gold, a store of value with a fixed supply of 21 million coins."],["Critics’ view","Critics argue that most cryptocurrencies have no real value beyond speculation, that their volatility makes them poor money, and that they are used for scams, money laundering and ransomware payments. Bitcoin mining’s electricity use has been compared to that of entire countries. The collapse of the FTX exchange in 2022, whose founder was later convicted of fraud, showed the risks of unregulated platforms."],["Governments and crypto","Countries take different approaches. El Salvador adopted Bitcoin as legal tender in 2021, though it later made its use voluntary as part of a loan agreement with the International Monetary Fund. China banned crypto trading and mining. The United States approved Bitcoin exchange-traded funds in 2024, making it easier for ordinary investors to buy. Many central banks, including in Asia, are studying their own digital currencies."]],
sp:["The bitcoins in the landfill","In 2013 a computer engineer in Wales accidentally threw away an old hard drive containing the private key to about 8,000 bitcoins that he had mined years earlier, when they were nearly worthless. The drive ended up buried in a local landfill. As Bitcoin’s price soared, the coins became worth hundreds of millions of dollars. He spent years asking the city council for permission to dig through the landfill, offering to share the money, but the council refused, citing environmental risks. His story became a famous warning about the importance of keeping private keys safe, and about how strange the world of cryptocurrency can be."],
g:[["Cryptocurrency","Digital money secured by cryptography"],["Blockchain","A shared public record of transactions"],["Private key","A secret code that controls cryptocurrency"],["Volatility","Large and rapid price changes"]],
f:["Bitcoin was launched in 2009.","Its creator’s identity is unknown.","Cryptocurrency prices are highly volatile."],
fun:"Two pizzas bought for 10,000 bitcoins in 2010 would later have been worth hundreds of millions of dollars."});

;
/* data/modern10.js */
// The Modern World: micromobility, cosplay, telemedicine and volunteering.
ADDART({id:"micromobility",t:"E-bikes, Scooters and Micromobility",c:"Transport",i:"🛴",w:"Micromobility",r:["bicycle","ev","megacities","smartcities","jeepney","sharingeconomy"],
p:[["Small and electric","Micromobility means small, light vehicles such as e-bikes and scooters."],["City travel","They are used for short trips in crowded cities."],["New rules","Cities are making new rules for where they can go."]],
l:"Micromobility refers to small, lightweight vehicles for short trips, usually carrying one or two people at low speeds, such as bicycles, electric bicycles, electric scooters, electric tricycles and small electric motorbikes. Their popularity has grown rapidly since the 2010s, driven by cheaper batteries, app-based sharing services and congestion in big cities. Supporters say micromobility can reduce traffic, air pollution and greenhouse emissions, and provide affordable transport. Critics point to accidents, sidewalk clutter, theft and unclear rules. In the Philippines, e-bikes and e-trikes became especially common after the COVID-19 pandemic, as people looked for cheaper alternatives to crowded public transport, prompting authorities to set new rules on registration, licenses and which roads they may use.",
s:[["Types of vehicles","Micromobility includes ordinary bicycles, pedal-assist e-bikes that boost a rider’s pedaling, throttle e-bikes that move without pedaling, standing e-scooters, electric skateboards, and small three-wheeled e-trikes often used for errands and deliveries. Some are owned, while others are rented through apps."],["Sharing systems","Bike-sharing schemes let people pick up a bicycle at one place and leave it at another. Early versions appeared in Europe, and large systems such as Paris’s Vélib’, launched in 2007, inspired cities worldwide. In the late 2010s, dockless bikes and scooters, unlocked with smartphones and left anywhere, spread rapidly from China to the Americas and Europe."],["Benefits","Small electric vehicles use far less energy than cars and take up little road and parking space. For short trips of a few kilometers, they can be faster than cars in traffic. They can help connect people to train and bus stations, solving what planners call the last-mile problem."],["Safety concerns","Riders of bikes and scooters are vulnerable in traffic, especially where there are no protected lanes. Injuries have risen in some cities as scooter use grew. Lithium battery fires in poorly made or improperly charged e-bikes have caused deadly fires in homes in several countries."]],
k:[["Examples","E-bikes, e-scooters, e-trikes"],["Typical trip","A few kilometers"],["Famous bike-share","Vélib’, Paris, 2007"],["Philippine rules","LTO classifications and road restrictions"],["Key safety issue","Lack of protected lanes"]],
d:[["1965","Amsterdam activists launch free white bicycles"],["2007","Paris launches the Vélib’ bike-sharing system"],["2017","Dockless e-scooter companies expand in the United States"],["2020","Pop-up bike lanes spread during the COVID-19 pandemic, including in Metro Manila"],["2021","The Philippines classifies electric vehicles by size and speed"],["2023","Paris voters choose to ban rental e-scooters"]],
s2:[["E-bikes and e-trikes in the Philippines","During and after the pandemic, many Filipinos bought e-bikes and e-trikes for commuting, deliveries and family errands, because they are cheaper to run than motorcycles or cars and do not require fuel. In 2021 the Land Transportation Office issued rules classifying electric vehicles by size and speed, with some requiring registration and licenses. In 2024 Metro Manila authorities banned e-bikes and e-trikes from major roads, citing safety, which supporters welcomed and many riders criticized as hurting low-income commuters."],["Bike lanes","In 2020 the Philippine government and cities built hundreds of kilometers of bike lanes in Metro Manila, Cebu and Davao, as bicycles became a lifeline when public transport was limited. Cycling groups have pushed for protected lanes separated from cars, while some motorists complain that lanes reduce road space."],["Bike graveyards","In 2016 and 2017, dozens of companies flooded Chinese cities with millions of dockless rental bicycles. Many companies went bankrupt, and huge piles of abandoned bikes, called bike graveyards, appeared on city outskirts. The episode showed the risks of rapid growth without planning."],["Paris says no to scooters","In April 2023 Paris held a public vote on whether to keep rental e-scooters. Although turnout was low, a large majority voted to ban them, and they were removed later that year. Supporters said the scooters were dangerous and cluttered sidewalks, while rental companies and some users said they were a useful, clean way to travel. Rental e-bikes remain popular in the city."],["Battery safety","Lithium-ion batteries power most e-bikes and scooters. Cheap or damaged batteries, or charging with the wrong charger, can cause overheating and fires. Fire departments advise buying certified products, not charging overnight or near exits, and never modifying batteries."],["The future of city travel","Urban planners see micromobility as part of a shift toward cities with fewer cars, combining walking, cycling, public transport and shared vehicles. Others argue that micromobility mostly replaces walking and public transport trips rather than car trips. Its impact depends on safe infrastructure and good planning."]],
sp:["Amsterdam’s white bicycles","In 1965 a group of young activists in Amsterdam called the Provos proposed a radical idea: the city should ban cars from its center and provide free white bicycles for everyone to use. To show how it would work, they painted a few dozen bicycles white and left them unlocked around the city for anyone to ride. Police soon confiscated many of the bikes, saying unlocked bicycles invited theft, and others were stolen. The experiment did not last, but it planted an idea. Decades later, bike-sharing systems inspired partly by the white bicycles spread to cities around the world, including Asia."],
g:[["Micromobility","Small, light vehicles for short trips"],["E-bike","A bicycle with an electric motor"],["Dockless","Able to be left anywhere rather than at a station"],["Last mile","The final short part of a journey"]],
f:["Micromobility includes e-bikes and scooters.","Paris launched Vélib’ in 2007.","Lithium battery safety is an important concern."],
fun:"Paris voted to remove its rental e-scooters in 2023, but rental bikes stayed."});

ADDART({id:"cosplay",t:"Cosplay",c:"Culture",i:"🎭",w:"Cosplay",r:["anime","manga","comics","videogames","superhero","fanfiction"],
p:[["Costume play","Cosplay means dressing up as characters from stories."],["Craft and performance","Cosplayers make costumes, props and wigs and act out roles."],["A global hobby","Cosplay events are held around the world, including in the Philippines."]],
l:"Cosplay, short for costume play, is a hobby in which people dress up as characters from anime, manga, comics, video games, films, television series and books, often making their own costumes, props and wigs and posing or performing as the characters. The word was coined in Japan in the 1980s, but fans had dressed as characters at science fiction conventions in the United States since the 1930s. Today cosplay is a global culture with conventions, competitions, photography and online communities, and some cosplayers have become professional performers and influencers. The Philippines has a large and active cosplay community, with events held in Metro Manila, Cebu, Davao and other cities. Cosplay combines sewing, painting, engineering, makeup and acting into a creative art.",
s:[["Making costumes","Many cosplayers build their costumes by hand, using fabric, foam, thermoplastics, 3D printing, paint and electronics. Armor may be cut from foam mats and sealed with paint, while lights and moving parts may be added with small motors. A complex costume can take months to complete."],["Performance","Cosplay is not only about clothing. Cosplayers study a character’s poses, expressions and personality, and some perform short skits on stage. Photographers work with cosplayers on photo shoots, and some cosplayers create videos and social media content."],["Conventions","Fan conventions bring together cosplayers, artists, game makers and fans. Events range from small local meet-ups to huge gatherings such as San Diego Comic-Con in the United States and Comiket in Tokyo. Many conventions hold cosplay contests judged on craftsmanship and performance."],["Inclusivity","Cosplay welcomes people of all ages, body types and backgrounds. Some cosplayers create gender-swapped or original versions of characters. Communities have worked to make events welcoming and safe for everyone."]],
k:[["Meaning","Costume play"],["Word coined","1984, Japan"],["Early roots","US science fiction conventions, 1939"],["International contest","World Cosplay Summit, Japan"],["Philippine scene","Large and active community"]],
d:[["1939","Fans wear costumes at the first World Science Fiction Convention in New York"],["1975","Comiket, a huge Japanese fan event, begins"],["1984","Japanese writer Nobuyuki Takahashi coins the word cosplay"],["2003","The World Cosplay Summit begins in Nagoya, Japan"],["2010s","Social media turns some cosplayers into global stars"],["2020s","Cosplay events grow across the Philippines"]],
s2:[["The birth of the word","In 1984 Japanese writer Nobuyuki Takahashi attended a science fiction convention in Los Angeles and was impressed by fans in costumes. Writing for a Japanese magazine, he combined the English words costume and play to create kosupure, or cosplay. The word spread in Japan with the growing popularity of anime and manga, and later around the world."],["Cosplay in the Philippines","Filipino fans of anime, games and comics have built one of Southeast Asia’s liveliest cosplay communities since the 2000s. Conventions and gaming expos in malls and convention centers attract large crowds, and some Filipino cosplayers have won international competitions and become well-known online personalities. Cosplay groups also join charity events and school activities."],["Cosplay is not consent","As cosplay grew, some cosplayers, especially women, reported being harassed or photographed without permission at events. In the 2010s, campaigns with the slogan cosplay is not consent encouraged attendees to ask before taking photos or touching costumes. Many conventions now have clear codes of conduct and staff to handle complaints."],["Skills for life","Many cosplayers say the hobby taught them sewing, design, engineering, painting, photography and public speaking. Some have turned these skills into careers in costume design, props for films and theater, special effects makeup or content creation."],["Cosplay and copyright","Cosplayers usually portray characters owned by companies. Most companies welcome cosplay as free promotion and fan appreciation, but questions arise when cosplayers sell photos or products featuring copyrighted characters. Some cosplayers create original characters to avoid these issues."],["Historical costume","Dressing up as characters is not new. Masquerade balls in Europe, costumes in festivals and theater, and children’s dress-up traditions all share similarities with cosplay. In the Philippines, festivals such as the Moriones Festival in Marinduque feature participants in elaborate costumes portraying Roman soldiers."]],
sp:["A year of foam and paint","A college student in Cebu decides to cosplay as a knight from her favorite video game at a convention next year. She studies screenshots and draws patterns on paper. For months, she cuts pieces of foam floor mats, heats and shapes them, glues them together and seals them with layers of paint to look like weathered metal. She learns to wire small lights into the helmet. Friends help her with the cape and wig. On convention day, she walks the hall in her finished armor, stopping for photos with fans who recognize the character. She does not win the contest, but she is already planning next year’s costume and has made a dozen new friends."],
g:[["Cosplay","Dressing up as a character from a story"],["Convention","A large gathering of fans"],["Prop","An object used in a costume or performance"],["Code of conduct","Rules for respectful behavior at events"]],
f:["The word cosplay was coined in 1984.","Cosplayers often make their own costumes.","Cosplay is popular in the Philippines."],
fun:"Fans wore costumes at a science fiction convention in 1939, decades before the word cosplay existed."});

ADDART({id:"telemedicine",t:"Telemedicine",c:"Health",i:"🩺",w:"Telehealth",r:["covid19","internet","digitaldivide","aimedicine","wearables"],
p:[["Doctor at a distance","Telemedicine lets patients consult doctors through phones and computers."],["A pandemic boom","Its use grew rapidly during COVID-19."],["Reaching islands","It can help people in remote areas get medical advice."]],
l:"Telemedicine, also called telehealth, is the use of telecommunications technology, such as phone calls, video calls, messaging apps and remote monitoring devices, to provide medical care at a distance. It allows patients to consult doctors without traveling to clinics, helps specialists advise health workers in remote places, and lets doctors monitor patients’ conditions at home. Early forms date back to medical advice given by radio and telephone, and space agencies monitored astronauts’ health from the ground. Telemedicine grew enormously during the COVID-19 pandemic, when lockdowns kept people at home. In the Philippines, with its thousands of islands and shortage of doctors in rural areas, telemedicine is seen as a way to extend health care, though limited internet access and privacy concerns remain challenges.",
s:[["How it works","A telemedicine consultation can be a video call where a doctor asks questions, observes the patient and gives advice or prescriptions. Patients may send photos of rashes or wounds, results of blood tests, or readings from home devices such as blood pressure monitors and glucose meters. Doctors can also consult specialists remotely."],["Benefits","Telemedicine saves travel time and costs, reduces crowding in hospitals and clinics, and lowers the risk of spreading infections. It is especially helpful for people in remote areas, people with disabilities, older adults and those needing regular follow-up for conditions such as diabetes or mental health."],["Limitations","Doctors cannot perform physical examinations or many tests remotely, so some conditions may be missed. Poor internet connections and lack of devices limit access, especially for poorer and rural families. Protecting patients’ privacy and data is also essential."],["Remote monitoring","Wearable devices and home sensors can send information about heart rate, blood sugar or oxygen levels to health workers, alerting them to problems early. Such monitoring is increasingly used for patients with chronic diseases and after hospital discharge."]],
k:[["Also called","Telehealth"],["Tools","Phone, video, messaging, sensors"],["Big boost","COVID-19 pandemic, 2020"],["Main benefit","Care at a distance"],["Main challenge","Internet access and privacy"]],
d:[["1920s","Ships at sea receive medical advice by radio"],["1960s","NASA monitors astronauts’ health from Earth"],["1990s","Internet-based telemedicine projects begin"],["2019","The Philippines passes the Universal Health Care Act"],["2020","Telemedicine use surges during the COVID-19 pandemic"],["2020s","Governments set rules for online consultations and prescriptions"]],
s2:[["Telemedicine in the Philippines","Doctors are concentrated in big cities, while many island and mountain communities have few or none. Philippine universities, hospitals and the Department of Health have run telehealth programs linking rural health workers with specialists. During the pandemic, many doctors and companies began offering online consultations, and authorities issued guidelines on practicing telemedicine safely and protecting patient data."],["The pandemic shift","In 2020, as lockdowns closed clinics and hospitals filled with COVID-19 patients, doctors around the world turned to phone and video consultations. In some countries, telemedicine use increased many times over in a few months. After the pandemic, many patients and doctors continued using it for routine follow-ups."],["Doctors in orbit","Space agencies developed some of the earliest telemedicine systems. In the 1960s, NASA monitored astronauts’ heart rates, breathing and temperatures from mission control. Astronauts on the International Space Station today consult doctors on Earth and use ultrasound guided remotely by experts."],["Concerns","Some doctors worry that online consultations can be rushed, miss important signs or lead to overprescribing. Patients may also receive advice from unqualified sources online. Health authorities stress that telemedicine should be provided by licensed professionals and that emergencies require in-person care."],["Mental health online","Telemedicine has made counseling and therapy more accessible, especially for people who live far from mental health professionals or feel uncomfortable visiting clinics. In the Philippines, hotlines and online counseling services expanded during the pandemic."],["Artificial intelligence","Some telemedicine platforms use artificial intelligence to help sort patients, answer basic questions or analyze images. Supporters say this can save doctors’ time, while critics caution that AI tools can make mistakes and must be carefully tested and supervised."]],
sp:["A consultation across the sea","On a small island in the Visayas, a midwife at the barangay health station is worried about a pregnant woman with high blood pressure and swelling. The nearest hospital with an obstetrician is a long boat ride away, and the sea is rough. Using a tablet and the station’s internet connection, the midwife starts a video call with a specialist at a provincial hospital. She shares the woman’s blood pressure readings and test results. The doctor advises immediate medicine and arranges for the woman to be transferred when the weather allows. Stories like this show how telemedicine can help bridge the distances in an archipelago, though it depends on reliable connections and trained health workers."],
g:[["Telemedicine","Medical care provided at a distance"],["Telehealth","Health services delivered using technology"],["Remote monitoring","Tracking health data from a distance"],["Consultation","A meeting with a doctor for advice"]],
f:["Telemedicine grew rapidly during COVID-19.","It helps people in remote areas.","It cannot replace all in-person examinations."],
fun:"Some of the earliest telemedicine systems were built to watch over astronauts in space."});

ADDART({id:"volunteering",t:"Volunteering",c:"Society",i:"🤝",w:"Volunteering",r:["phtyph","disasterprep","sdgs","loneliness","family","philanthropy"],
p:[["Helping freely","Volunteers give their time to help others without pay."],["Community spirit","The Filipino spirit of bayanihan celebrates community help."],["Good for volunteers too","Studies suggest volunteering can improve well-being."]],
l:"Volunteering means freely giving time and effort to help others or a cause without expecting payment. Volunteers work in hospitals, schools, disaster relief, environmental clean-ups, sports, religious organizations, animal shelters and many other areas. Some volunteer occasionally, while others serve full-time for months or years in their own countries or abroad. Volunteering has deep roots in many cultures. In the Philippines, the tradition of bayanihan, in which neighbors work together to help one another, such as by carrying a family’s house to a new location, is a celebrated symbol of community spirit. Volunteers are especially important after disasters such as typhoons and earthquakes. The United Nations celebrates International Volunteer Day every December 5.",
s:[["Kinds of volunteering","Volunteers may help directly, such as tutoring children, serving meals or visiting the elderly, or indirectly, such as organizing events, raising funds or offering professional skills like medicine, law or design. Online or virtual volunteering lets people help from home, for example by translating documents or mapping disaster areas."],["Disaster volunteers","In disasters, volunteers pack relief goods, cook meals, search for missing people, give first aid and help rebuild homes. Organizations such as the Red Cross train volunteers in advance. After Typhoon Haiyan, known locally as Yolanda, struck the Philippines in 2013, thousands of volunteers from the country and abroad helped in the response."],["Why people volunteer","People volunteer to help others, support causes they care about, learn new skills, meet people and gain experience for jobs. Many religious traditions encourage service to others. Young people often volunteer through schools and youth organizations."],["Benefits for volunteers","Research suggests that volunteering can improve volunteers’ mood, sense of purpose and social connections, and may be linked to better health and longer life, especially among older adults. However, volunteers can also face stress and burnout, especially in difficult work such as disaster response."]],
k:[["Meaning","Freely giving time to help others"],["Filipino tradition","Bayanihan"],["International Volunteer Day","December 5"],["Philippine law","Volunteer Act of 2007"],["Famous organization","The Red Cross"]],
d:[["1863","The International Committee of the Red Cross is founded"],["1961","The US Peace Corps begins sending volunteers abroad"],["1985","The UN establishes International Volunteer Day"],["2007","The Philippines passes the Volunteer Act"],["2013","Volunteers respond to Typhoon Yolanda"],["2021","Community pantries spread across the Philippines"]],
s2:[["Bayanihan","The word bayanihan comes from bayan, meaning town or community. The classic image of bayanihan shows a group of villagers lifting a nipa hut on bamboo poles and carrying it to a new place, with the family preparing food for the helpers afterward. The term is used today for any community effort, and the Philippine government named laws for pandemic response after it."],["Community pantries","In April 2021, during the COVID-19 pandemic, a young woman set up a small cart of free food on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City with a sign inviting people to give what they could and take what they needed. The idea spread quickly, and hundreds of community pantries appeared across the country. The movement was praised as an example of bayanihan, though some pantries faced crowding and controversy."],["Volunteering abroad","Programs such as the US Peace Corps, Japan’s JICA volunteers and the United Nations Volunteers send volunteers to work in other countries, including the Philippines. Filipino volunteers also serve abroad. Critics of short-term voluntourism argue that some trips benefit volunteers more than communities, and recommend programs that respect local needs and skills."],["Volunteering and the law","The Philippine Volunteer Act of 2007 recognizes volunteering as an important part of national development and sets out the roles of government, schools and organizations in supporting volunteers. The Philippine National Volunteer Service Coordinating Agency helps coordinate volunteer programs."],["Young volunteers","Many Filipino students volunteer through school community service programs, youth groups, scouting and church organizations. Some take part in tree planting, coastal clean-ups and literacy programs. The National Service Training Program in college includes community service components."],["Digital volunteers","After disasters, online volunteers around the world help map damaged areas using satellite images, verify information on social media and translate messages. During Typhoon Haiyan, volunteers mapped buildings and roads in affected areas to help relief agencies plan their responses."]],
sp:["The pantry on Maginhawa Street","On April 14, 2021, as the pandemic left many families without income, a young entrepreneur named Ana Patricia Non placed a bamboo cart on Maginhawa Street in Quezon City. She filled it with rice, canned goods and vegetables, and hung a sign: give according to your ability, take according to your need. Within days, neighbors began adding food, and people lined up to take what they needed. Photos spread on social media, and soon community pantries appeared in towns and cities across the Philippines, run by volunteers, churches, students and businesses. For many, the pantries became a symbol of the bayanihan spirit in hard times."],
g:[["Volunteer","A person who works freely without pay"],["Bayanihan","The Filipino spirit of community cooperation"],["Relief goods","Food and supplies given after disasters"],["Voluntourism","Travel combined with volunteer work"]],
f:["International Volunteer Day is December 5.","Bayanihan means community cooperation.","Community pantries spread in the Philippines in 2021."],
fun:"The classic picture of bayanihan shows neighbors carrying a whole house on bamboo poles."});

